---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { houses } from '../../../data/houses';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The 12 Houses', href: '/explore/houses/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The 12 Houses: areas of experience"
  description="The twelve houses describe areas of life and experience. Learn what each house covers, why birth time matters, and why a house means more in relation to the planets and signs within it."
  path="/explore/houses/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>The 12 houses</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Houses describe areas of experience: where the different parts of a chart are most visibly
        expressed. They help move astrology from general symbolism into the actual arenas of a life,
        and their placement depends on birth time and the house system being used.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.houses.src}
        srcset={scenes.houses.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.houses.alt}
        width={scenes.houses.width}
        height={scenes.houses.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-8);">
      <h2>The house is the setting, not the whole story</h2>
      <p>
        A house tells us <em>where</em> something is operating, but not by itself what the experience
        will look like. A planet in the 7th house brings its function into one-to-one relationships;
        the sign, aspects, ruler, condition, and the rest of the chart tell us far more about how
        that may be lived. The same house emphasis can show up very differently in two people.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is also one of the clearest places to see why exact birth time matters. The Ascendant
        anchors the chart's house structure, so uncertainty in the time can change which houses some
        planets occupy. When the time is uncertain, a careful astrologer should say what is solid and
        what is not.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container grid grid--3">
      {
        houses.map((h) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">{h.number}</span>
            <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
              <a href={`/explore/houses/${h.slug}/`}>{h.name}</a>
            </h2>
            <p class="card__meta">{h.traditionalName}</p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">{h.covers[0]}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>How houses fit into the whole chart</h2>
      <p>
        House positions are tied to <a href="/explore/angles/">the four angles</a>, and astrologers
        may use different <a href="/explore/house-systems/">house systems</a>. That can change some
        house placements without changing the planets' zodiac positions. Rather than treating that
        as a reason to panic about which system is "right," I think it is more useful to understand
        what each system is doing and then interpret consistently within it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
